{"name":"Smoke & Ash","hex":"#939789","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smoke","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible vapor/vapour, gases, and fine particles given off by burning or smoldering material.","A cigarette."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To inhale and exhale the smoke from a burning cigarette or cigar, burning material in a pipe, etc.","To inhale and exhale tobacco smoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoke"},{"term":"ash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Abishira."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The solid remains of a fire.","The nonaqueous remains of a material subjected to any complete oxidation process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Smoke is an aerosol emitted when a material undergoes combustion or pyrolysis, together with the quantity of air that is entrained or otherwise mixed into the mass. It is commonly an unwanted by-product of fires, but may also be used for pest control (fumigation), communication, defensive and offensive capabilities in the military, cooking, or smoking. It is used in rituals where incense, sage, or resin is burned to produce a smell for spiritual or magical purposes. It can also be a flavoring agent and preservative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg/330px-Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke"},{"title":"Ampersand","blocks":[{"text":"The ampersand, also known as the and sign, is the logogram &, representing the conjunction \"and\". It originated as a ligature of the word et."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Et_sign.svg/330px-Et_sign.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ampersand"},{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chemical composition distribution of volatile organic compounds released in smoke from a variety of solid fuels[4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmoke_chemical_composition.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emission of soot in the fumes of a large diesel truck, without particle filters","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiesel-smoke.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Wildfiretopanga.jpg/500px-Wildfiretopanga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Wildfiretopanga.jpg/1280px-Wildfiretopanga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm